    "text": "Power option setting for the highest performance\nSelect Control Panel → Hardware → Power Options. In the window, select the High\nperformance Windows power plan option, as shown in Figure 6-14.\n-Cortrol Perel -Harcvrare -Power Options\nSeerch Control Panel\nControl Panel Home\nSelect a power plan\nChoose whet the power button\nPower plans can heip you maximze your corputers performance or conserve energy.\ndoes\nMae a plan acbive by selecting it, or choose a plan and customize it by changing tc pcvrer\nsettings. Tell me more about power plans\nCreste a power plan\nChoose when to turn off the\nPreferred plers\ndisplay\n Bolonccd (rccommended)\nChange plan sotirgs\nAutomatically balances performance with energy consumption on\ncapable hardware.\nHigh performance\nChange plan settrgs\nFavors performance, but may use more energy.\nSee also\nUser Accounts\nShow additional plars\nFigure 6-14 Windows Power Options\nOptimum disk command timeout settings\nAdjust the disk TimeOutValue parameter on the Windows operating system for more reliable\nmultipath access.\nWindows operating systems feature a default disk command TimeOutValue of 60 seconds. lf a\nscSl command does not complete, the application waits 60 seconds before an l/O request is\ntried again. Because this behavior can create issues with most applications, you must adjust\nthe disk TimeOutValue in the registry key to a lower value. For more information about setting\nthis value, see the Microsoft TechNet website at\nhttps://technet.microsoft.com/en-us/library/aa997069(v=exchg.80).aspx.\nAdjust the following key to your needs:\nHKLM\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Disk\\TimeOutValue\nConsider the following points:\nFor a disk in a nonclustered configuration, set TimeOutValue to 10.\n7\nFor a disk in a clustered configuration, set TimeOutValue to 20.\n6.3.7 FlashSystem 900 and client VMware ESX hosts\nThe FlashSystem 90o can be attached to VMware ESX client hosts by using the FC method.\nArbitrated loop topology is required when you attach the IBM FlashSystem 90o directly to the\nclient VMware ESX hosts.\nNote: The FlashSystem 900 16 Gbps FC attachment does not support arbitrated loop\ntopology. The IBM FlashSystem 900 must be connected to a SAN switch when 16 Gbps\nFC is used if the host operating system does not support point-to-point FC direct\nconnections. If arbitrated loop is required by the client host, connect at 8 Gbps FC to the\nIBM FlashSystem 900.\nChapter 6. IBM FlashSystem 900 client host attachment and implementation\n171",
